tweet: "Climate and Conflict Collide as Indigenous Leaders Press the UN on Land Rights"
---
Indigenous leaders gathered at the United Nations this week, hundreds of delegates attending the 19th annual session of the UN Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, to address a compounding threat to Indigenous land rights and lives. The threat is not solely environmental, but a convergence of climate breakdown, armed conflict, state pressure, and legal marginalization that bear hardest on communities with the least capacity to absorb them. ([ICT News / Grist, July 16](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/))

## A Declaration and the Distance It Has to Travel

The UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, adopted nearly two decades ago by over 100 countries, was designed to protect the rights and lives of Indigenous peoples worldwide. The gap between that adoption and actual protection is where the current crisis is concentrated. Despite the declaration's existence, record-breaking storms still batter Indigenous lands, drought still desiccates them, and seas continue to rise, causing territories to vanish. [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/) reports that the declaration's existence has not paused any of these environmental disasters.

EMRIP â€” one of three UN bodies focused exclusively on Indigenous peoples â€” is composed of seven Indigenous experts, each representing a different region, and operates within the UN Human Rights Council to develop data, recommendations, and advice on international standards for Indigenous rights. According to [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/), the 19th annual session brings together Indigenous leaders, governments, and experts for a week of discussions covering both longstanding and emerging issues, including inadequate disaster relief, artificial intelligence, and resource extraction.

## Climate Is Not the Only Front

The climate threats documented in [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/) do not operate in isolation. Indigenous peoples continue dying by the hundreds in wars and while defending their lands. Government persecution compounds this picture further. All three forces â€” climate breakdown, armed conflict, and state pressure â€” are running simultaneously against the same communities, not sequenced across separate timelines or distinct geographies.

"Climate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization reinforce one another," said Binota Moy Dhamai, who is Tripura from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh and a former chair of EMRIP. This framing names the structural dynamic the Geneva gathering exists to address: not separate crises running in parallel, but interlocking pressures that compound each other's damage.

For Indigenous peoples in the Pacific, climate change and colonization are inextricably linked. Valmaine Toki, who is MÄori and EMRIP's member representing the Pacific region, said conflict leads to the dispossession and alienation of Indigenous peoples from their traditional lands even as those same people are drawn into the fighting. "It's a lose-lose situation for Indigenous peoples on all fronts," Toki told [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/).

## What Indigenous Leaders Are Demanding of States

Bringing these crises to Geneva rather than to individual national capitals signals the scale at which Indigenous leaders see this problem â€” and where they locate the failure of authority. The 19th session exists, as [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/) reports, explicitly to discuss implementing solutions to these abuses, not only to document them. Whether this record translates into anything material depends on whether the states that adopted the declaration treat it as a constraint on their own development and security decisions.

The struggles of Indigenous Newar communities in Nepal illustrate what that gap looks like on the ground. According to [Cultural Survival](https://www.culturalsurvival.org/news/nepals-new-governments-fast-track-project-priority-sparks-conflict-over-indigenous-newar-lands), Nepal's new government has designated the Kathmandu-Tarai Fast Track expressway a top national priority, placing it in direct conflict with Indigenous Newar communities in Khokana and Bungamati. Those communities are demanding that the government respect free, prior, and informed consent, remove army camps installed on their land without that consent, and shift the expressway's starting point south â€” an alternative that the National Indigenous Nationalities Commission, Lalitpur Metropolitan City, and the Department of Archaeology have each formally recommended.

In northern Ontario, the Canadian government has granted mining rights on the Breathing Lands, a vast wetland ecosystem that forms part of the Ring of Fire mineral deposit, despite strong resistance from Nishnawbe Aski Nation, which represents 49 First Nations communities. Kohen Mattinas of Lac Seul First Nation and Constance Lake First Nation, representing Nishnawbe Aski Nation at EMRIP, told [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/) that the right of all Indigenous peoples to free, prior, and informed consent must be respected. Mattinas co-founded Okiniwak in 2025 in response to Canadian legislation that allows projects to be advanced through an accelerated process.

Although challenges vary across regions, those gathered in Geneva this week are pressing a common set of demands: that governments consult and include Indigenous peoples in decisions affecting their lands, that UNDRIP be treated as an enforceable standard rather than an aspirational text, and that emerging threats such as artificial intelligence be subject to Indigenous data sovereignty protections. The approaching 20th anniversary of the declaration's adoption frames the stakes. As [ICT News / Grist](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/) reports, the central question facing delegates remains the same as it was in 2007: not whether Indigenous rights should be recognized, but whether governments are prepared to uphold them.

